    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The work presents the Conquest from a viewpoint highly favourable to William, and modern scholars generally read it as an apologia for it, though Harold and his soldiers are shown as brave.</li><li>Its maker and place of making are uncertain: Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> are the usual view, with Edith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, Saint-Florent at Saumur and Barking Abbey among other proposals.</li><li>It survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in 1562, use as a wagon cover in 1792 and German interest during the occupation, and is the sole surviving example of medieval narrative needlework.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The last surviving caption, &quot;Et fuga verterunt Angli&quot; (&quot;and the English retreated&quot;), is said to be entirely spurious, added shortly before 1814 at a time of anti-English sentiment.</p></aside>
    </section>

