    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1077</span><span class="rf-text">The tapestry was possibly completed in time to be displayed at the dedication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> Cathedral</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1476</span><span class="rf-text">An inventory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> Cathedral gives the earliest known written reference to the hanging</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1729</span><span class="rf-text">Bernard de Montfaucon began publishing drawings and a description of the whole work</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1792</span><span class="rf-text">It was confiscated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> to cover military wagons, and a local lawyer rescued it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1944</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> took it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, and Himmler's order to remove it to safety came too late</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2007</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added it to the Memory of the World International Register</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2026</span><span class="rf-text">It travelled outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for the first time, on loan to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings.jpg" alt="Battle of Hastings" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene57_Harold_death.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Myrabella, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The tapestry tells the story of the events of 1064 to 1066 that culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> had no children or clear successor, and succession was decided jointly by the king and the assembly of nobles, the Witenagemot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and the most powerful noble in England, was Edward's brother-in-law; William, Duke of Normandy, was the great-nephew of Edward's mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a>. The Norman chronicler William of Poitiers reported that Edward had named William his heir and that Harold had sworn to honour this, a claim other sources such as Eadmer dispute.</p><p>Narrative hangings were not unusual. Tapestry fragments from ninth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, such as those in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship">Oseberg ship</a> burial and at Överhogdal, are thought to stand behind Norman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> embroidery. The Liber Eliensis mentions a hanging commemorating the deeds of Byrhtnoth, killed in 991, and English and Norman texts praised the skill of Anglo-Saxon seamstresses. A poem by Baldric of Dol describes a similar hanging in the apartments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adela-of-normandy">Adela of Normandy</a>. The Bayeux Tapestry is notable as the sole survivor of this kind of work.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Bayeux Tapestry&#8221; cites where it covers Origins, Recorded history, Historical accuracy:</p><ul><li>Lucien Musset, The Bayeux Tapestry (Boydell Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-count-of-ponthieu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guy-i-count-of-ponthieu.jpg" alt="Guy I, Count of Ponthieu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-count-of-ponthieu">Guy I, Count of Ponthieu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guy_of_Ponthieu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bayeux Tapestry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The narrative opens with Edward sending Harold to Normandy. Harold lands in the wrong place and is taken prisoner by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-count-of-ponthieu">Guy I of Ponthieu</a>, then handed to William, whom he accompanies on a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conan-ii-duke-of-brittany">Conan II, Duke of Brittany</a>; near Mont Saint-Michel he saves two soldiers from quicksand. William gives Harold arms, and Harold swears an oath on relics, though the tapestry gives no clue to what was promised. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Edward dies, apparently leaving the crown to Harold, who is crowned with Stigand, the excommunicated archbishop, in attendance. Halley's Comet appears, a fleet is built, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> land, and the battle follows, ending with Harold's death and the English in flight.</p><p>The surviving piece depicts 623 human figures, 994 animals, 438 plants, 37 buildings and 41 ships. The central zone holds the action, with scenes divided by stylised trees; the borders carry birds, beasts, fables, farming, hunting and nude figures, and recent scholarship suggests they offer a parallel commentary. Stephen D. White has argued that the fables, drawn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aesop">Aesop</a> through Phaedrus, comment on predation and betrayal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> tituli name people and places, at times with English spelling.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/canterbury"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canterbury.jpg" alt="Canterbury" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury-cathedral-wyrdlight.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Antony McCallum: Who is the uploader, photographer, full copyright owner and proprietor of WyrdLight.com, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The work is technically an embroidery: crewel wool on a tabby-woven linen ground 68.38 metres long and 70 centimetres tall, of which about 50 centimetres carry the design. Outline or stem stitch was used for lettering and outlines, and couching or laid work for filling figures, in ten natural dye colours, mainly terracotta, blue-green, dull gold, olive green and blue. Nine linen panels were embroidered separately and sewn together; at the first join the borders do not line up, but later joins are almost invisible. Analysis by the French National Centre for Scientific Research found dyes from madder, weld and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>.</p><p>Scholarly analysis in the 20th century concluded that it was probably commissioned by Bishop Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>, who appears four times, possibly for the dedication of his cathedral in 1077. Most scholars place its making in or around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>. Howard B. Clarke proposed in 2012 that the designer was Scolland, abbot of St Augustine's, Canterbury, and Christine Grainge has suggested Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanfranc">Lanfranc</a>. Carola Hicks suggested Edith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> as patron, George Beech the Abbey of Saint-Florent at Saumur, and Janina Ramirez the nuns of Barking Abbey.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>An inventory of 1476 shows the hanging displayed every year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> Cathedral during the week of the Feast of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, still the practice in 1728. By the 18th century its art was thought crude, even barbarous. Antoine Lancelot reported a sketch of part of it in 1724, and Bernard de Montfaucon traced it to Bayeux and published drawings by Antoine Benoît in 1729 and 1730. William Stukeley noted it in English in 1746, and Smart Lethieullier's account appeared in 1767.</p><p>Its exhibition at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> caused a sensation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s time and inspired a musical play in 1804; with the planned invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> abandoned, it went back to Bayeux. It became a tourist attraction: John Ruskin called it "the most interesting thing in its way conceivable", while Robert Southey complained of queueing to see it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a> thought it "the work of amateurs". Modern readers have found propaganda in it, and Andrew Bridgeford has suggested it hides English messages against Norman rule. Historians debate whether the figure with an arrow in the eye is Harold, since early engravings show a spear there, and the meaning of the scene of a cleric and a woman named Ælfgyva is unknown.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler.jpg" alt="Heinrich Himmler" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_Himmler_(3x4_cropped)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Friedrich Franz Bauer, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The tapestry survived the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in 1562. In 1792 it was seized to cover military wagons and rescued by a local lawyer. A linen backing was sewn on in 1724, scene numbers were inked on it around 1800, and later generations patched it and reworked parts, especially the final scene; the end has been missing from time immemorial. Charles Stothard's facsimile, published by the Society of Antiquaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1819 to 1823, records it before the 19th-century restoration.</p><p>It was stored for safety in 1870 and again from 1939 to 1944. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> coveted it, and fragments taken from its underside in 1941 were found in 2023 and returned in 2026. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> moved it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> in June 1944, but when the SS tried to take it on 22 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was again in French hands. After the liberation it was shown at the Louvre on a 70-metre picture rail late in 1944, then returned to Bayeux in March 1945 and has been on display at the Séminaire de Bayeux since 1983. In 2026 it was lent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, despite a petition by Didier Rykner that the journey might damage the fabric.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Replicas have been made in many media. Elizabeth Wardle and some 37 women of the Leek School of Art Embroidery stitched a full-size copy in 1885 and 1886, now in Reading Museum; others followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, the last a half-scale mosaic of 1.5 million pieces of spring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> made by Michael Linton over twenty years. In 2024 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> Museum bought one of three known surviving Victorian copies. Jan Messent in 1997 and the people of Alderney in 2013 made continuations ending with William's coronation, the subject the missing panels may have shown.</p><p>Its strip-like narrative has made it a model for comics: Scott McCloud cited it in Understanding Comics, and Bryan Talbot called it "the first known British comic strip". It inspired cartoons such as John Hassall's Ye Berlyn Tapestrie of 1915, embroideries such as the Overlord Embroidery of 1974 and the Prestonpans Tapestry of 2010, and the work of Hayao Miyazaki, and films such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> and The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> have used it in their titles. Historians still use it as a visual record of 11th-century arms, clothing, castles and ships, and it is the first known depiction of Halley's Comet and of a harrow.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Bayeux Tapestry is a linen embroidery nearly 70 metres long that tells the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman conquest of England</a> from 1064 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a>. Probably commissioned by Bishop Odo and made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, it presents a Norman view of events, yet remains a unique record of 11th-century life and the only surviving medieval narrative needlework.</em></p>

